<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    AkariTool.App.Tests - the App-layer test project (TEST-01, plan 01-01).

    Shape: mirrors tests/AkariTool.Infrastructure.Tests/AkariTool.Infrastructure.Tests.csproj
    exactly - same 5-property PropertyGroup, same five PackageReference versions, all of
    which are already in the local NuGet cache. The one difference is the single
    ProjectReference below (pointing at AkariTool.App rather than Core+Infrastructure).

    SCOPE FENCE (D-04): this project's Phase 1 remit is SettingBadgeCalculatorTests ONLY.
    The real-catalog validity test is Phase 2 / BUG-02; the container-resolution test is
    Phase 3 / TEST-02; the Core-references test is Phase 3 / TEST-03. Adding any of them
    here would move another phase's requirement AND destabilise the recorded baseline test
    count that nine later phases compare against.

    UseWinUI was TRIED AND REMOVED - measured, not assumed (D-01's letter is therefore not
    honoured; D-01's stated rationale is preserved). Setting UseWinUI=true here compiles and
    copies the App reference fine, but it breaks the TEST RUN itself: with UseWinUI=true the
    five Microsoft.TestPlatform.* assemblies that vstest's testhost loads at run time are NOT
    copied to this project's output, so every test run aborts with
      "Unhandled exception. System.IO.FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly
       'Microsoft.TestPlatform.CoreUtilities, Version=15.0.0.0'"
    and vstest reports "Test Run Aborted" with no tests at all. With UseWinUI absent the
    same clean rebuild copies all five and the smoke test runs and passes.

    This is the documented fallback, and it costs nothing here: SettingBadgeCalculator needs
    no XAML, no visual tree and no live DispatcherQueue, so D-01's actual constraint -
    "tests only App logic that needs no live DispatcherQueue and no XAML visual tree" - is
    fully preserved. What is given up is the letter of UseWinUI=true, which existed only to
    support XAML that this project never uses. <EnableMsixTooling> and <WindowsPackageType>
    were NOT added: the markup targets never demanded them once UseWinUI was gone.

    NOTE the separate solution-file consequence, which is NOT caused by UseWinUI: this
    project references an x64-only assembly (AkariTool.App has Platforms=x64 and
    RuntimeIdentifier=win-x64), so it must be mapped to the real x64 platform in
    AkariTool.sln. Built as AnyCPU it fails with "error MSB3270: There was a mismatch
    between the processor architecture of the project being built MSIL and the reference
    AMD64". That is why this project's output path carries an x64 segment while the other
    two test projects' does not.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="8.7.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="NSubstitute" Version="5.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE reference, to AkariTool.App. Do NOT also reference Core or Infrastructure:
    AkariTool.App already references both, and SettingBadgeCalculator reaches
    NumericConversionHelper transitively through the App reference. Explicitly no
    InternalsVisibleTo anywhere - SettingBadgeCalculator is already public, so the
    attribute would be an unused widening of the shipping assembly's surface.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/AkariTool.App/AkariTool.App.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>